  "worked_solution": "Recall that: \n\n- the product of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the product of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Expression I is irrational. Since ${10}^{12}$ is rational and $\\sqrt{5}$ is irrational, their product must be irrational.\n\n- Expression II is irrational. We have $\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{{13}^{5}}=\\frac{1}{{13}^{5}}\\cdot \\sqrt{6},$ and since $\\frac{1}{{13}^{5}}$ is rational and $\\sqrt{6}$ is irrational, their product must be irrational.\n\n- Expression III is irrational. Since $3.777$ is rational and $\\sqrt{31}$ is irrational, their product must be irrational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
